<main><section class="hero"><div class="orb" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="hero-content"><span class="eyebrow">A declaration for the living north</span><h1>Let the north<br>remain <em>wild.</em></h1><p class="intro">The North Frigid Zone.<br>A vision for rewilding, collective stewardship and nature reserve status under Midguardr Realm, with the Rasniki (<i>Homo lunarus</i>) species at the heart of its fictional setting.</p><div class="status">Proposed designation</div><br><a class="button" href="#declaration">Read the declaration &nbsp; ↗</a></div><div class="coordinates">90° N — 66°33′51.1″ N · NORTH FRIGID ZONE</div></section>
<div class="stats" aria-label="Regional facts"><div><strong>90° N</strong><span>The North Pole · northern boundary</span></div><div><strong>≈ 4.12%</strong><span>Of the Earth's surface</span></div><div><strong>24 hours</strong><span>Seasonal sunlight or darkness</span></div></div>
<section class="section" id="declaration"><div><p class="eyebrow" style="color:#56754e">01 / The declaration</p><h2>A reserve for<br>the living world.</h2></div><div><p class="declaration">“Midguardr Realm declares its vision for the North Frigid Zone to be rewilded, nationalised into collective stewardship, and recognised as a nature reserve dedicated to the continuity of Arctic life.”</p><p>Within this fictional setting, the Rasniki (<i>Homo lunarus</i>) species is named as part of Midguardr Realm’s vision of collective stewardship. The name is used as a creative species designation, rather than an established scientific classification.</p><p>Through this declaration, Midguardr Realm calls for the protection of polar habitats, the recovery of damaged ecosystems, and a future in which the wellbeing of the living north guides decisions about its care.</p><p class="note">This is a statement of aspiration by Midguardr Realm. It does not establish legal nationalisation, ownership, sovereignty or an officially recognised nature reserve, and does not alter existing jurisdictions or Indigenous rights.</p></div></section>
<div class="principles" id="rewilding"><section class="section"><div><p class="eyebrow" style="color:#56754e">02 / Rewilding commitments</p><h2>Protection.<br>Recovery.<br>Continuity.</h2></div><div><article class="principle"><span class="number">01</span><div><h3>Protect connected habitats</h3><p>Support the conservation of Arctic seas, tundra and coastlines, with space for wildlife and the natural processes that sustain it.</p></div></article><article class="principle"><span class="number">02</span><div><h3>Restore with care</h3><p>Prioritise the recovery of human-damaged habitats through evidence-led restoration appropriate to each place. Rewilding here means caring for native ecosystems, with no assumption that the whole region needs intervention.</p></div></article><article class="principle"><span class="number">03</span><div><h3>Stewardship with people</h3><p>Ground any future conservation work in Indigenous leadership, local participation, consent and respect for existing rights, alongside ecological research.</p></div></article></div></section></div>
<section class="section" id="region"><div><p class="eyebrow" style="color:#56754e">03 / The region</p><h2>Beyond the<br>Arctic Circle.</h2></div><div><p>The North Frigid Zone extends from the Arctic Circle to the North Pole. Using the latitude given in the referenced geographical-zone description, its southern boundary is 66°33′51.1″ N; its northern boundary is 90° N. It accounts for approximately 4.12% of Earth's surface.</p><p>The region experiences extreme polar cold and seasonal periods when the sun remains above or below the horizon for 24 hours or longer, depending on latitude and season.</p><p class="source">Geographical reference: <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geographical_zone" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Geographical zone — Wikipedia ↗</a>. The Arctic Circle's exact latitude changes over time; the coordinate above follows the supplied reference.</p></div></section>
